steps.body:
The best-known security hole in web apps comes from one habit: building a query by pasting a user's input into the SQL text. This part shows the attack, the one fix that always works, and then how a row from the database becomes one of your own types.

## SQL injection

Say the login endpoint finds a user by email, and builds the query by joining strings:

```java sketch
// Never do this.
jdbc.sql("SELECT id, email, role FROM users WHERE email = '" + email + "'")
```

With `ada@roombook.example`, it sends the query it meant to. Now someone types `' OR '1'='1` as the email. The first quote closes the string early, and the rest becomes SQL: `WHERE email = '' OR '1'='1'`, which is true for every row, so the query returns every user. With a little more typing, an attacker can read other tables, or change and delete data. That's **SQL injection**, and it's been near the top of every list of web attacks for twenty years. The security lesson comes back to it.

## Parameters

The fix is to never put a value into the SQL text. You write a **placeholder** where each value goes, and pass the values separately:

```java sketch
jdbc.sql("SELECT id, email, role FROM users WHERE email = :email")
    .param("email", email)
```

Plain JDBC writes each placeholder as `?` and sets the values by position. `JdbcClient` also takes named placeholders like `:email`, which read better and can't get out of order. Either way, PgJDBC sends the values as parameters, and turns each placeholder into PostgreSQL's own numbered form, `$1`, `$2`, which is what the diagram shows.

```diagram raw-sql-injection
Pasted into the text, input becomes SQL. Sent as a parameter, it stays data.
```

The driver sends the SQL text and the values to PostgreSQL as separate parts of one message. PostgreSQL works out what the SQL means first, and only then fills in the values, as plain data. A value can hold quotes, semicolons, or the word DROP, and it's still just text to compare with an email. Nothing is escaped, so there's nothing to escape from.

Joining pieces of SQL that you wrote in the code is fine, and part 5 does it to add filters. The rule is that a value from outside the code, from a request, a file, or even your own database, never becomes part of the SQL text. Placeholders only hold values, though. A table name, a column name, or a word like DESC can't be a parameter, so when the request picks one, like a sort order, you choose it from a fixed list in your code. Part 5 shows how.

## From a row to your type

Under `JdbcClient` is plain JDBC, Java's standard database interface, and it's worth seeing once:

```java sketch
try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
     PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT name FROM rooms WHERE id = ?")) {
  ps.setLong(1, roomId);
  try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
    return rs.next() ? Optional.of(rs.getString("name")) : Optional.empty();
  }
}
```

A **PreparedStatement** is the SQL with its placeholders, and a **ResultSet** walks through the rows. try-with-resources gives the connection back to the pool even when something throws. Every query needs that ceremony, so Spring wraps it. With `JdbcClient` you write the SQL and its parameters, and a **RowMapper** turns each row into a record:

```java sketch
@Repository
class JdbcBookingRepository implements BookingRepository {
  private static final String COLUMNS = """
      id, room_id, user_id, starts_at, ends_at,
      attendees, note, status, version, created_at""";

  private static final RowMapper<Booking> BOOKING = (rs, rowNum) -> new Booking(
      rs.getLong("id"),
      rs.getLong("room_id"),
      rs.getLong("user_id"),
      rs.getObject("starts_at", OffsetDateTime.class).toInstant(),
      rs.getObject("ends_at", OffsetDateTime.class).toInstant(),
      rs.getInt("attendees"),
      rs.getString("note"),
      BookingStatus.valueOf(rs.getString("status").toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT)),
      rs.getInt("version"),
      rs.getObject("created_at", OffsetDateTime.class).toInstant());

  private final JdbcClient jdbc;

  JdbcBookingRepository(JdbcClient jdbc) {
    this.jdbc = jdbc;
  }

  @Override
  public Optional<Booking> findById(long id) {
    return jdbc.sql("SELECT " + COLUMNS + " FROM bookings WHERE id = :id")
        .param("id", id)
        .query(BOOKING)
        .optional();
  }
}
```

Read `timestamptz` as `OffsetDateTime`, PgJDBC's type for it, and turn it into an `Instant`. `getString` returns `null` for NULL, but `getInt` and `getLong` return `0`, so a number that can be NULL needs `getObject(column, Long.class)`. `COLUMNS` is a constant written in the code, so adding it to the SQL text is safe, unlike the email above.

`.query(Booking.class)` can map rows without a RowMapper, matching each column to a record component and turning `room_id` into `roomId`. That works while every column has a simple type. Roombook's status is lowercase text while the enum's constants are capitals, and amenities are an array, so the next example shows what happens then.

## One row, or none

`.optional()` gives an empty `Optional` when no row matched, and the service turns that into the not-found problem from the validation lesson, a 404. `.single()` throws when there isn't exactly one row, so use it only when a missing row would be a bug.

examples.code:
-- What the code meant to send:
SELECT id, email, role FROM users
WHERE email = 'ada@roombook.example';

-- What it sent when someone typed  ' OR '1'='1  as the email:
SELECT id, email, role FROM users
WHERE email = '' OR '1'='1';

PREPARE find_user (text) AS
  SELECT id, email, role FROM users WHERE email = $1;

EXECUTE find_user('ada@roombook.example');

EXECUTE find_user(''' OR ''1''=''1');

record Room(long id, String name, int capacity, List<String> amenities) {}

jdbc.sql("SELECT id, name, capacity, amenities FROM rooms WHERE id = :id")
    .param("id", 1L)
    .query(Room.class)
    .optional();

