A list endpoint like `GET /v1/bookings?status=confirmed&from=2026-10-01` is where queries get interesting. Its filters are optional, so the SQL changes shape from one request to the next. The list comes back a page at a time. And each booking may need data from another table. This part builds the repository method behind it.

## Optional filters

Each filter the request sends adds one condition to the WHERE, with its value as a parameter. Building the WHERE from fixed pieces of SQL written in your code is safe. What varies is which pieces you use, and every value still travels as a parameter.

```java sketch
public List<Booking> listForUser(BookingQuery q) {
  var where = new ArrayList<String>(List.of("user_id = :userId"));
  var params = new HashMap<String, Object>(Map.of("userId", q.userId()));
  if (q.status() != null) {
    where.add("status = :status");
    params.put("status", q.status().name().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT));
  }
  if (q.from() != null) {
    where.add("starts_at >= :from");
    params.put("from", utc(q.from()));
  }
  if (q.to() != null) {
    where.add("starts_at < :to");
    params.put("to", utc(q.to()));
  }
  if (q.after() != null) {
    where.add("(starts_at, id) > (:afterStartsAt, :afterId)");
    params.put("afterStartsAt", utc(q.after().startsAt()));
    params.put("afterId", q.after().id());
  }
  params.put("limit", q.limit() + 1);
  return jdbc.sql("SELECT " + COLUMNS + " FROM bookings WHERE " + String.join(" AND ", where)
          + " ORDER BY starts_at, id LIMIT :limit")
      .params(params)
      .query(BOOKING)
      .list();
}
```

`BookingQuery` is a record of the parsed query string, plus the caller's id. Each filter adds a condition with a named placeholder and puts its value in the map, and `.params(params)` passes them all. Times go in as `OffsetDateTime` through a small `utc(instant)` helper, `instant.atOffset(ZoneOffset.UTC)`, since PgJDBC can't send an `Instant` as a parameter. The second example shows the error you get if you try.

## Pages with a cursor

The request data lesson chose **keyset pagination**, also called cursor pagination: each page starts right after the last row of the page before, found by the sort's columns. Roombook sorts bookings by `(starts_at, id)`. The id breaks ties, since two bookings can start at the same moment, and without it a page could skip one or show it twice. The condition `(starts_at, id) > (...)` compares the pair in order: a later start, or the same start with a higher id. With the `(user_id, starts_at)` index, PostgreSQL jumps straight to that spot, so page 500 is as fast as page 1. An `OFFSET 10000` would read and throw away ten thousand rows first.

The method asks for one row more than the page holds. If that extra row comes back, there's another page: the service drops it, and builds `nextCursor` from the last row it keeps. If not, `nextCursor` is null.

## Sorting from an allowlist

A placeholder can't hold a column name, so a `sort` parameter needs another approach. Map each value the API accepts to a fixed piece of SQL, and refuse anything else with a 422:

```java sketch
private static final Map<String, String> ROOM_SORTS = Map.of(
    "name", "name, id",
    "capacity", "capacity, id");

// The controller already refused any other sort with a 422.
String orderBy = ROOM_SORTS.get(sort);
return jdbc.sql("SELECT id, building_id, name, capacity FROM rooms ORDER BY " + orderBy + " LIMIT :limit")
    .param("limit", limit)
    .query(ROOM)
    .list();
```

The request picks a key, and the SQL comes only from your code.

## N+1, and how to avoid it

Say the bookings list shows each room's name. Fetching each booking's room one at a time is the N+1 pattern from part 1, and it's just as slow without an ORM. There are two fixes:

- **JOIN** the rooms in the same query, when you always need them together.
- **Fetch them all at once.** Collect the room ids from the page, and ask for all of them in one query with `WHERE id = ANY(:ids)`. PgJDBC sends a Java array, like `Long[]`, as a PostgreSQL array. JdbcClient also expands a `List` written as `IN (:ids)` into one placeholder per item.

```java sketch
public Map<Long, String> roomNames(List<Long> roomIds) {
  return jdbc.sql("SELECT id, name FROM rooms WHERE id = ANY(:ids)")
      .param("ids", roomIds.toArray(Long[]::new))
      .query((rs, rowNum) -> Map.entry(rs.getLong("id"), rs.getString("name")))
      .list()
      .stream()
      .collect(Collectors.toMap(Map.Entry::getKey, Map.Entry::getValue));
}
```

Either way, it's at most two queries, however long the page.

Writes are where the API changes data, and where the database's rules push back. This part covers INSERT for POST, UPDATE for PUT and PATCH, telling whether a write found its row, and turning the database's refusals into the API errors from the validation lesson. It ends with the swap this lesson has been building up to.

## INSERT ... RETURNING

A new booking gets its id, status, version, and timestamps from the table's defaults. **RETURNING** lists columns to send back from the rows a statement wrote, so one round trip saves the booking and returns it, ready for the 201 response:

```java sketch
public Booking insert(NewBooking b) {
  try {
    return jdbc.sql("""
            INSERT INTO bookings (room_id, user_id, starts_at, ends_at, attendees, note)
            VALUES (:roomId, :userId, :startsAt, :endsAt, :attendees, :note)
            RETURNING id, room_id, user_id, starts_at, ends_at,
                      attendees, note, status, version, created_at
            """)
        .param("roomId", b.roomId())
        .param("userId", b.userId())
        .param("startsAt", utc(b.startsAt()))
        .param("endsAt", utc(b.endsAt()))
        .param("attendees", b.attendees())
        .param("note", b.note())
        .query(BOOKING)
        .single();
  } catch (DataIntegrityViolationException e) {
    throw translate(e);
  }
}
```

## UPDATE for PUT and PATCH

A PUT replaces the whole resource, so its UPDATE sets every column the client may change. A PATCH sets only the fields the request sent, which means building the SET list the way part 5 built the WHERE. The routes lesson drew the line between a missing field, which means leave it alone, and a null one, which means clear it. Jackson reads a missing field and a null one both as `null`, so the request data lesson's handler reads the body as a `JsonNode` and asks `has()`, and the repository adds `note = :note` only for fields that were present. Both kinds add one to `version`, which the next lesson uses to catch two people editing the same booking at once.

## Did it find the row?

An UPDATE or a DELETE that matches no rows isn't an error to the database. It reports `UPDATE 0` and moves on, so the repository has to check the count. `JdbcClient`'s `.update()` returns the count as an `int`. Cancelling uses it: `WHERE id = ... AND status = 'confirmed'` changes one row the first time and none the second, so the method returns whether it did anything, and the service decides on the answer.

```java sketch
public boolean cancel(long id) {
  int changed = jdbc.sql("""
          UPDATE bookings
          SET status = 'cancelled', cancelled_at = now(), updated_at = now()
          WHERE id = :id AND status = 'confirmed'
          """)
      .param("id", id)
      .update();
  return changed == 1;
}
```

Deleting a room in Roombook is a soft delete, `UPDATE rooms SET active = false`, so its old bookings still point at a real row. A real `DELETE FROM` is for rows nothing points at.

## When the database says no

Every PostgreSQL error carries a **SQLSTATE**, a five-character code that names the problem the same way in every driver and every language. The ones an API meets most start with 23, the class for broken rules:

| SQLSTATE | Name | Roombook example | Answer |
|---|---|---|---|
| `23505` | unique_violation | a second account for one email | 409 |
| `23503` | foreign_key_violation | a booking for room 99, which doesn't exist | 422 |
| `23514` | check_violation | a room with a capacity of 50 | 422 |
| `23P01` | exclusion_violation | two bookings that overlap, from the next lesson | 409 |
| `23502` | not_null_violation | a value the code forgot to send | 500, a bug |

Validation at the edge should catch bad values before they get this far, so a 23514 usually means a check is missing from the schema. A 23505 or a 23P01 is different: only the database can see all the other rows, so those errors are the normal way to learn about a conflict. The error also names the rule that failed, like `bookings_room_id_fkey`, so one SQLSTATE can lead to different answers depending on the rule.

Spring turns the driver's `SQLException` into its own exceptions: `DuplicateKeyException` for 23505, and the more general `DataIntegrityViolationException` for the other broken rules. The SQLSTATE is on the driver's exception underneath, which `getMostSpecificCause()` reaches:

```java sketch
private static RuntimeException translate(DataIntegrityViolationException e) {
  String state = e.getMostSpecificCause() instanceof SQLException sql ? sql.getSQLState() : null;
  return switch (state) {
    case "23503" -> new UnprocessableException("room-not-found", "That room doesn't exist.", e);
    case "23P01" -> new ConflictException("booking-overlap", "The room is already booked then.", e);
    case null, default -> e;
  };
}
```

The repository turns the errors it expects into domain errors, so nothing above it knows about the driver. Anything else goes up unchanged, and the central error handler answers 500 and logs the details.

## The swap

That's the repository, apart from `countUpcoming`, one `SELECT count(*)` over the member's upcoming confirmed bookings, which the next lesson writes. `JdbcBookingRepository` implements the `BookingRepository` interface from the lesson on layers, now with `listForUser` and `cancel` added to it, and carries `@Repository`, so Spring hands it to `BookingService`'s constructor in its place. The in-memory class loses its annotation and moves to `src/test`, where the testing lesson uses it to check the service without a database. The service never changed a line, and never learned that the data now lives in PostgreSQL. The next lesson, Constraints, Triggers, Hooks, and Transactions, makes the database itself refuse bad data, double bookings included.

